WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.830
Film Courage: Why writers should educate themselves
throughout their careers?

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.390
What are they using for those tools [to continually
educate themselves]?

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:10.270
Jen Grisanti: I love that you asked that question!

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:18.369
So the interesting thing is I have taught
all over the world.

00:00:18.369 --> 00:00:28.820
I have found in countries like Israel, Australia
and cities like Toronto and London that the

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:40.060
type of writers that attend story events and
ongoing education are working writers versus

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:41.840
in the U.S.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:51.670
The story events that are like the bigger
story events that are massive, are for usually

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:59.370
I would say, the higher percentage of attendees
are writers across the U.S. who had an idea

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:03.600
and want to make it happen versus working
writers.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:13.749
And I definitely believe that this holds writers
back from creating longevity in their career.

00:01:13.749 --> 00:01:18.749
I believe a writer should always be educating
themselves.

00:01:18.749 --> 00:01:24.680
I always say about myself that I am a teacher
for a moment and a student for life and I

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:35.569
am constantly reading every book that comes
out on story and promotion and business and

00:01:35.569 --> 00:01:44.920
anything that can make me move to a higher
level, spiritually, work-wise, on a personal

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:45.920
level.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:53.939
Like I am all about the idea that we have
to constantly…everyday I wake up and think

00:01:53.939 --> 00:01:57.289
about how can I be better than I was yesterday?

00:01:57.289 --> 00:02:05.369
And for the writer, it really is how can I
expand my knowledge of story through continuous

00:02:05.369 --> 00:02:15.250
learning, as well as my observance and absorption
of life and being able to communicate that

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:16.620
story.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:21.310
How can I continuously do that in a way that
will fuel my career?

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:26.230
Film Courage: Do you think it’s Western
culture and the emphasis on over-confidence

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:27.230
[that plays into this] a little bit?

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.580
Whereas in other cultures it’s okay to be…

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:30.790
Jen Grisanti: I do.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:31.819
I do.

00:02:31.819 --> 00:02:37.829
I sit there and I think…I look at these
story events that have all the top story people

00:02:37.829 --> 00:02:46.360
in the business, many of whom you’ve interviewed,
and I think “Why would you not take the

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:49.410
opportunity to continuously learn?”

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:52.430
I’ve been in the business 25 years.

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:54.939
I have seen every side to the business.

00:02:54.939 --> 00:03:03.719
And I always think why would you not take
a sounding board…many of these writers…I

00:03:03.719 --> 00:03:07.589
mean when you look at Michael Hauge, you look
at Chris Vogler who’ve had incredible careers…you

00:03:07.589 --> 00:03:20.469
look at John Truby and Eric Edson…there
is so much that they have to say that brings

00:03:20.469 --> 00:03:25.129
value to the story process and that’s important.

00:03:25.129 --> 00:03:34.190
So ongoing education in my view, is the key
to creating a writing career that will last.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:39.069
Film Courage: So just to re-iterate, in the
States, you’ve seen many what you call ‘Baby

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:42.180
Writers’ (and I think you used that term
and it doesn’t refer to their age.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:47.219
It’s in terms of experience) but then outside
the States, Europe and beyond, these are working

00:03:47.219 --> 00:03:49.280
writers and they are willing to…

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:55.079
Jen Grisanti: I’ll ask the question at the
beginning of an event “How many of you have

00:03:55.079 --> 00:03:56.099
sold a pilot?”

00:03:56.099 --> 00:03:59.999
or “How many of you are currently on staff?”

00:03:59.999 --> 00:04:09.390
And everywhere else it is mind blowing to
see two-thirds of the room raise their hands

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:16.340
versus here you’d maybe see 10 people raise
their hands.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:19.870
And it’s a very different thing.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:23.210
I don’t know why that is?

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:29.370
Now I can say with my personal events because
I’ve worked with so many working writers,

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:32.550
I get writers of all levels show up at those
events.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:40.310
But when I do these big story events where
there are 20 or 30 speakers, (in my world)

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.169
I’m like “Are you kidding me?

00:04:43.169 --> 00:04:44.340
That’s incredible!”

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.900
I want to soak it up.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:53.199
Another one of my favorite story people is
Dara Marks, I look at it and I want to soak

00:04:53.199 --> 00:04:58.960
up everything they have learned and how they
see story and how it aligns with how I see

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:03.780
story because that makes me a better teacher.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:13.960
And I always love seeing how we coin things
in different ways and it comes from our world

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.340
view and how we teach story.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:25.330
And I think what we’re all trying to master
is teaching story in a way that leads the

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:28.650
writer to sell the story.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:34.449
Film Courage: In terms of questions, do you
see a difference in the type of question and

00:05:34.449 --> 00:05:42.259
even the tone that you get in the U.S. based
groups versus…you know how there are always

00:05:42.259 --> 00:05:46.850
certain people in a class that want to debate
the teacher, whereas those who have a healthy

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:49.710
curiosity and they want to know the answer
[laughs].

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:56.610
Jen Grisanti: Well one thing I found that’s
interesting between the audiences…I definitely

00:05:56.610 --> 00:06:03.490
think the U.S. audiences absolutely have a
curiosity and I think that people want to

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:04.490
learn.

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:10.889
I definitely think that in the U.S. we’re
connected with the idea of the ‘Quick Fix.’

00:06:10.889 --> 00:06:12.129
I have an idea.

00:06:12.129 --> 00:06:14.340
I want to sell it and make it work.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.639
And that’s definitely part of it.

00:06:16.639 --> 00:06:24.979
Where the writer in Australia does not make
the type of money that the writer in the U.S.

00:06:24.979 --> 00:06:25.979
makes.

00:06:25.979 --> 00:06:32.090
Like many Australian writers end up coming
here because they don’t make the kind of

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:39.380
money but they get the foundation there and
then they come here.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:46.129
I think that there is a tone in the questions
definitely from different countries based

00:06:46.129 --> 00:06:51.000
on the world view of how the writer is seen
in that country.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:59.340
I mean we are very fortunate that the writer
is seen in a very strong light in the TV world

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:03.180
and the Film world, even though in the TV
world the writer has more power.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:10.180
So I think it causes writers from around the
world to want to get a foundation in their

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:14.930
own culture and succeed there and then come
here.

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:22.960
I definitely have noticed at a number of events
I’ve done internationally that the writers

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:30.919
who are…I mean I remember in Australia I
met one writer who had like 4 shows but was

00:07:30.919 --> 00:07:38.729
still wanting to come to the U.S. and wanting
to expand because they viewed the U.S. as

00:07:38.729 --> 00:07:44.960
the end all, be all because of the pay and
because of the way people see writers.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:50.479
Film Courage: Right…Do you think film and
television writing is open to all class and

00:07:50.479 --> 00:07:53.800
education levels, whereas with the literary
world (especially in the let’s say the East

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:57.400
Coast) it’s more of a closed network?

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:02.310
Jen Grisanti: Yeah, definitely.

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.800
I mean the literary world, it’s interesting.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:14.069
I have worked with several novelists, not
near the about that I have with TV and feature

00:08:14.069 --> 00:08:15.970
[film] writers because that’s my background.

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:22.160
I mean I definitely know that story is story
and you’re going to benefit and learn from

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:23.160
anyone.

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:27.430
Certainly there are more closed off communities
then how they see.

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:35.430
But all I also see a massive trend right now
for a screenwriter writing a book and then

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:37.180
writing a screenplay.

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:45.940
So I think that is opening up the idea and
I personally love the process because I love

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:58.010
the idea of really deeply going into an internal
story in the book and then showing that in

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:03.050
visual and action in the adaption into the
screenplay.

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:08.070
Film Courage: So then it creates this whole
new viewer which is the one who reads the

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:11.030
book first and then they want to see it and
weigh it against the novel.

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:12.030
Jen Grisanti: Yes.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:16.860
And then the writer has more power, too, when
they write the book and then sell the film.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:20.079
Film Courage: I’m thinking of writer Janet
Fitch who wrote White Oleander.

00:09:20.079 --> 00:09:23.700
Which was a book someone recommended to me
but I never read it.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:25.910
And then I absolutely loved the movie!

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:30.510
And now I’m almost afraid to read the book
but for me I had been recommended the book.

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:34.260
Jen Grisanti: And that also happens, are you
an internal person?

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:40.850
The reason I am not a screenwriter is because
I am an internal writer and I know that about

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:42.600
myself, you know?

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:43.839
Film Courage: …Going back to self-awareness.

00:09:43.839 --> 00:09:44.839
Jen Grisanti: Yep…

00:09:44.839 --> 00:09:45.250
Question for the Viewers: What have you learned
the most about screenwriting?

